		)
		(uuid "2c52a69b-fc4a-497b-b23a-b6aa74413e65")
	)
	(text_box "* Q: Why 24V? Why not just connect the N.O. stomp pad cable pair directly to GPIO?\n   A: Noise Immunity. A GPIO with a pull-up is a high impedance node. Over a ~10ft run it acts as an antenna, able to pick up noise and trigger false stomps. Driving ~9mA through a low impedance 24V loop instead makes the signal far harder to corrupt, now noise must overcome real current and not just a floating voltage. The 24V loop decision then led to choosing the H11L1M for both isolation and a clean bounce-free edge for the GPIO with the built-in Schmitt trigger.\n\n* Two 1k resistors used in the 24V H11L1M emitter loop to split the power draw in 2, allowing for a smaller package size. Power draw for one resistor is given by:\n\nI_F = (24V - V_F(H11L1M) - V_F(LED) ) / R \n   = (24V - 1.5V - 3.2V) / (1k + 1k) = 9.65mA\nP = I_F ^ 2 * R = (9.65 mA) ^ 2 * (1k) = 0.093 W\n\n* For future versions, place the indicator LED in \nparallel to the optocoupler LED. In this version's \nconfiguration, they share the same current (wired in \nseries), but if they were to be wired in parallel, \nthey'd share the same voltage, making it easier to \nsize resistors for each individual LED's optimal \ncurrent rating.\n\n* 1N4148 diode used for reverse polarity protection "
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 200.66 66.9191 0)
		(size 63.5 61.1979)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
